{"author":"rebar review bot","author_email":"rebar-review-bot@rebar.solutions.navateam.com","data":{"body":"Investigation complete. Two corrections to the working assumptions, one of them\nblocking.\n\nRETRACTION FIRST: an earlier investigation pass reported the current plan as\n\"1 to add, 1 to change, 11 to DESTROY\", including destruction of the live\nBedrock spend-cap budget, Lambda, SNS topic and IAM role. That is WRONG and is\nwithdrawn. It was produced by running terraform plan from the shared checkout,\nwhich is 28 commits behind origin/main and predates the commit that restored\ninfra/terraform/bedrock_spend_cap.tf. The stale tree genuinely lacked those\nresources, so the plan proposed destroying them.\n\nRe-run from a worktree pinned at origin/main (5aa0eef98fdf), the plan is exactly\nthe set this ticket describes:\n\n  aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm.reviewbot_webhook_auth_rejections  will be created\n  aws_iam_role_policy.bedrock_spend_cap                          update in-place\n  aws_instance.gerrit                                            update in-place\n  aws_lambda_function.bedrock_spend_cap                          update in-place\n  aws_sns_topic_policy.bedrock_spend_cap_alerts                  update in-place\n  Plan: 1 to add, 4 to change, 0 to destroy.\n\nZero destroys. No resource is replaced.\n\nPER-ITEM SAFETY\n\nItem 1 (webhook auth-rejection alarm) - SAFE, purely additive. The alarm does\nnot exist in CloudWatch today, so there is no live alarm to disturb; its\nalarm_actions point at the existing aws_sns_topic.alerts.\n\nItems 2 and 4 (narrow OrganizationAccountAccessRole) - SAFE. The role reports\nRoleLastUsed {} (never used), and the IAM service-last-accessed report shows\nBedrock with TotalAuthenticatedEntities 0. Nothing is exercising the grant being\nnarrowed.\n\nItem 3 (gerrit instance) - SAFE, no replacement. The verb is update in-place;\nuser_data_replace_on_change is unset and defaults to false, and the lifecycle\nblock at infra/terraform/main.tf:103 carries only ignore_changes = [ami]. The\nnew user_data takes effect on the next boot, not on apply.\n\nItem 5 (SNS topic policy) - NOT SAFE AS WRITTEN. This is the blocking finding.\n\nThe diff retains the AllowBudgetsPublish statement for budgets.amazonaws.com and\nREMOVES the AllowCostAlerts statement for costalerts.amazonaws.com. Those are two\ndifferent AWS services, and the second one is live:\n\n  aws ce get-anomaly-subscriptions\n    bedrock-anomaly-alerts | frequency IMMEDIATE\n      -> SNS arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:896586841071:bedrock-spend-cap-alerts\n\nAWS Cost Anomaly Detection publishes under the costalerts.amazonaws.com service\nprincipal, so applying item 5 revokes the only grant that lets that subscription\ndeliver. The bedrock-anomaly-alerts subscription would stop reaching the topic,\nand it would fail silently: publish is denied at the topic, not at the\nsubscription, so the subscription stays green in the console while delivering\nnothing.\n\nThat grant is real drift, but it is drift in the correct direction - the\nAllowCostAlerts statement exists in AWS and does not exist in the Terraform\nsource. The remedy is to codify it, not to revoke it: add the AllowCostAlerts\nstatement to the aws_sns_topic_policy.bedrock_spend_cap_alerts document so the\nsource matches the live, working policy. After that the item-5 diff disappears\non its own.\n\nSo: items 1-4 are safe to apply as-is; item 5 needs the Terraform source amended\nbefore any apply, or the apply will break live immediate Bedrock spend-anomaly\nalerting.\n\nScope note: terraform-drift.yml only runs plan and reports. It never applies, so\nnothing here is at risk of landing unattended."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789930865834490001,"uuid":"ddcae8d6-b3ab-48f5-b811-a515b2604561"}